RRFS CI Design and Usage Guide
Lin Gan

This document outlines the design pattern and usage of the automated GitHub Actions CI pipelines established to enforce NCEP Central Operations (NCO) Implementation Standards.

Core Design Pattern

Every new CI test adheres to a strict standard to maintain uniformity:

Workflow File (.yml): Named NCO_Consistency_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.yml. Controls the GitHub Action runner.

Exclusion List (.lst): Named exclude_file_list_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.lst. Used by repository admins to permanently bypass specific files from tests.

Regression Test (.sh): Named regression_test_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.sh. Verifies that the testing logic works correctly on known bad data.

Zero-Match Logic: If a repository branch lacks matching target files, the CI completes successfully (exit 0) but clearly logs "No files found to check. Exiting gracefully" in ci_run.log.

Artifact Generation: Output is logged to ci_run.log and uploaded as an artifact, as well as displayed in the $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY.

Available Pipelines

1. Yamllint (NCO_Consistency_Yamllint)

Target: .yml, .yaml files.

Purpose: Validates basic syntax, indentation, format constraints (120 character line length max), trailing spaces.

2. Yamllint Scientific Hierarchical (NCO_Consistency_Yamllint_Scientific_Hierarchical)

Target: .yml, .yaml files.

Purpose: Utilizes a custom Python PyYAML wrapper (check_hierarchical_yaml.py) to enforce scientific structural standards, specifically throwing hard errors if duplicate keys are detected within a block (which normally overwrite silently).

3. Implementation Standard (NCO_Consistency_Implementation_Standard)

Target: Bash scripts (.sh) in ./jobs, ./scripts, ./ush.

Purpose: Enforces NCO EE2 checklist requirements via linter_nco_implementation_standard.py.

No hardcoded paths (/lfs, /gpfs).

No redirecting to > /dev/null.

Enforces directory structure (./jobs must be uppercase, ./scripts lowercase ex*.sh).

Validates err_chk follows mpiexec/aprun.

Prevents rm -rf $DATA outside of the j-job conditional logic.

Skipping & Disabling Checks

1. Global Skip (via Commit/PR Message)

You can disable an entire CI job globally by including ci-ignore: <job_name> in your commit message or PR description.

Example: git commit -m "Updating docs. ci-ignore: yamllint"

Available tags: yamllint, yamllint_scientific, implementation_standard.

2. File-level Skip (via Inline Comment)

Place a comment in the specific file to bypass checks for that file only.

Example in YAML: # ci-ignore: yamllint_scientific

Example in Bash: # ci-ignore: implementation_standard

3. Repository-level Skip (via List)


Admins can permanently exclude files or directories by adding their relative paths to the corresponding .lst file in .github/workflows/.